= Author Biographies

== Georgios Varnavides

Georgios Varnavides received dual B.S. degrees in Materials Science and Engineering and Civil and Environmental Engineering from Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 2017.
He completed his Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering at MIT in 2022, with a thesis titled "Electron Hydrodynamics in Crystalline Solids".
He received the Materials Research Society Graduate Student Gold Award in Fall 2021.
From 2022 to 2025, he was a Miller Research Fellow at University of California, Berkeley, where he developed novel methods for low-dose computational electron microscopy.
He is currently Assistant Professor of Imaging Physics at Delft University of Technology, where he leads the Curious Beams Lab in advancing electron imaging and instrumentation -- particularly at lower accelerating energies -- to probe structural and functional properties of complex systems.

== Willem P.M. de Kleijne

Willem P.M. de Kleijne began his Ph.D. in Imaging Physics at Delft University of Technology in November 2025.
He received an M.Sc. in Econometrics from Tilburg University in 2024 and an M.Sc. in Applied Physics from Eindhoven University of Technology in 2025.
During an internship at ASML's Research Department, he developed a strong interest in advanced imaging techniques at the interface of mathematics and physics.
His current research focuses on developing coherent diffractive imaging in a scanning electron microscope for both transmission and reflection geometries.

== Stephanie M. Ribet

Stephanie M. Ribet received her B.A. in Chemistry, with a minor in Materials Science, from Princeton University in 2016.
From 2016 to 2018, she worked as a chemist at Dyson/Sakti3 on solid-state battery development.
She earned her Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering from Northwestern University in 2023, designing nanocomposite sponges and characterizing them across multiple length scales. 
In her final doctoral year, she was an Office of Science Graduate Student Research Fellow at the Molecular Foundry, developing STEM methods for soft and hybrid materials.
She received the Materials Research Society Graduate Student Gold Award in Fall 2022.
She subsequently continued at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory as a postdoctoral researcher advancing multidimensional high-resolution electron microscopy.
She joined the Molecular Foundry as a Staff Scientist in 2025.